<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sokollu Mehmed Pasha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1505">1505</a>–<a href="/years/1579">1579</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Mehmed-paša Sokolović, Sokollu Mehmet Paşa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sokollu Mehmed Pasha was an Ottoman statesman, grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> from 1565 to 1579 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-ii">Selim II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-iii">Murad III</a>. Born into an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christian family in Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>, he was taken as a boy through the devşirme and rose to be High Admiral of the Fleet, Governor-General of Rumelia and vizier. As grand vizier he kept Suleiman's death at Szigetvár secret in 1566 until Selim II could take power, made peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a> in 1568 and rebuilt the fleet after Lepanto. He helped restore the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbian-patriarchate-of-pec">Serbian Patriarchate of Peć</a> in 1557, endowed the eleven-arched bridge at Višegrad and was assassinated on 11 October 1579.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sokollu-mehmed-pasha.jpg" alt="Sokollu Mehmed Pasha" width="330" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1569 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sokollu_Mehmed_Pasha_(detail)_exploring_the_surroundings_of_Szigetvár._Nüzhet,_fol._22a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sokollu's birth name was probably Bajica, and he was of Bosnian Serb origin, said to have been born into a modest shepherd family near Sokolovići, in the vicinity of modern Rudo. His father was named Dimitrije. His name Sokollu is a demonym from his place of birth. His family relations are disputed: Makarije Sokolović is traditionally called his brother, but some historians think he was a nephew or distant relative. Renamed Mehmed, he was educated first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a> and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, as an apprentice <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissary</a> and at the palace school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a>. He spoke Turkish, Serbian, Persian, Arabic, Venetian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Mehmed became Imperial Chamberlain in 1541 and then head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>'s squires, growing close to Suleiman. He is said to have excelled at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a> and the first Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayreddin-barbarossa">Hayreddin Barbarossa</a> died in 1546 he became his successor as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapudan-pasha">Kapudan Pasha</a>, took part in the expedition against Tripoli and greatly strengthened the fleet's arsenal. In 1551 he became Governor-General of Rumelia, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>. In 1555 he was made Third Vizier, and in 1559 he defeated the forces of Prince Bayezid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> in a rebellion against the heir Selim; Bayezid fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and the Shah later handed him and his four sons over to be executed.</p><p>In 1562 Mehmed married Ismihan Sultan, a granddaughter of Suleiman, and in 1561 he had become Second Vizier. Suleiman made him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a> in June 1565, after the death of Semiz Ali Pasha. In 1566 war with Emperor Maximilian II began, and Mehmed prepared the army for the Sultan's advance, and his forces were sent to besiege Szigetvár. He served as Grand Vizier under Suleiman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-ii">Selim II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-iii">Murad III</a>. Although he became a Muslim he remembered his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> roots, and in 1557 he helped restore the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbian-patriarchate-of-pec">Serbian Patriarchate of Peć</a>, with Makarije Sokolović as its patriarch. He placed members of his family in important positions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sokollu Mehmed Pasha was assassinated on 11 October 1579. Some claim that the killer was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissary</a> in the pay of Safiye Sultan, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-iii">Murad III</a>. Afterwards Murad III changed grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> ten times in sixteen years, which is taken as a sign of the instability that followed. He endowed buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>, Halep, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, where his bridges are especially remembered, notably the eleven-arched bridge at Višegrad. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> the architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mimar-sinan">Mimar Sinan</a> built his mosque complex in Kadırga and his tomb at Eyüp, where he is buried.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sokollu Mehmed Pasha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Basil I, born to peasant parents, rose from groom to emperor in 867 in the Byzantine Empire; <b>Sokollu Mehmed Pasha</b>, born to a Serbian Orthodox family, governed the Ottoman Empire as grand vizier for fourteen years.</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sokollu Mehmed Pasha</b>, born an Orthodox Christian in Herzegovina, was grand vizier from 1565 to 1579.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sokolovići, Sokolac, in what is now <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.94,18.93&amp;year=1553" aria-label="Sokolovići, Sokolac on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5004 607 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sokollu Mehmed Pasha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5454 877h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5454 877h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.94,18.93&amp;year=1553">What was here in 1553? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
